Official feed test fixtures
===========================

Every file in this folder is SYNTHETIC: written by hand for these tests in the
shape of each publisher's real feed (element names, namespaces, date formats,
encodings and quirks as the feeds had them on 2026-10-18), with invented
items. None is a recording of a live response; every headline, summary, name,
link path, release number and GUID is made up.

One file per feed of the allow-list (src/marketlens_mcp/providers/official/
feeds.py), named <feed id> plus the document's kind:

* fed.*.xml: the Federal Reserve Board's RSS 2.0 (GMT dates, a category, the
  description repeating or extending the title, HTML entities escaped twice,
  no author element). Speech and testimony titles start "Word, ...": a
  speaker's surname, or not (the "Supervision, ..." testimony item), which is
  why no author is read from a title.
  fed.monetary.xml starts with a byte-order mark and uses CRLF line ends, as
  the Board's feeds do. Its FOMC item is in fed.press.xml too (dedupe).
* sec.*.rss: SEC's RSS 2.0 with dc:creator (blank, or a litigation release
  number), UUID guids, padded titles, links ending in a newline, and a
  description of escaped HTML with &nbsp; and a script element. One headline
  tries to give orders (it is data).
* bls.latest.rss: BLS's latest numbers, RSS 2.0 with xml:base and an escaped
  HTML table. bls.<release>.atom: BLS's Atom release feeds (id, link href,
  content, published and updated with fractional seconds and offsets); the
  CPI's first entry was updated after it was published.
* bea.news.rss: BEA's RSS 2.0 with http links (served as https) and one
  relative link.
* cftc.*.xml: the CFTC's RSS 2.0 (xml:base https://www.cftc.gov/) with empty
  descriptions and dc:creator holding a web editor's user name (press,
  enforcement) or the speaker (speeches); one item has a date that does not
  parse, one links off the CFTC's domain, one has a path-relative link that
  resolves against the xml:base, not the feed's URL.
* ecb.press.xml: the ECB's single-line RSS 2.0 without descriptions, links
  with a double slash, +0200 dates.
* efts.search-index.json: one page of SEC EDGAR's full-text search
  (efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index) in its JSON shape as it was on
  2026-10-20 (took, _shards, hits.total {value, relation}, hits with _id
  "<accession>:<file name>" and _source, aggregations, the query echoed),
  served for any query string. Every name, CIK, accession number, file
  number and place is invented. Its six hits: a 13F-HR cover page
  (primary_doc.xml), a 13F-HR/A, an 8-K exhibit (ex99-1.htm, file_type
  EX-99.1, items), a Form 144 listing the issuer, then the person (two
  display names, one without tickers), a Form D with no period_ending, and
  a hit whose file name holds a slash (skipped: it would be placed in a URL).

The golden outputs in ../golden/ are what news_official and filings_search
must produce from these fixtures (rows, response absent map, notes,
provenance minus fetched_at). Regenerate with MARKETLENS_UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 and
review the diff.
